# An unannotated parameter is `unknown` in SIR: every operation on it is a
# run-time check. A call that passes values of known types gets a clone whose
# parameters have those types, typed again inside (`fib[n:Scalar]` has no
# `unknown` value left, and returns a number). The recursive calls find the
# clone by name. The clone is per exact type, dimension included: `sq(3)`
# and `sq(3 m)` are different clones. `twice(true)` is an error at run time
# (`+` needs numbers), so a Bool clone would not verify: that call keeps the
# generic `twice`, and its error is unchanged.
def fib(n):
    if n < 2:
        return n
    return fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2)

def sq(x):
    return x * x

def twice(x):
    return x + x

print(fib(10), sq(3), sq(3 m), twice(2))
print(twice(true))
